# What each harness provides and what each gate requires. The single source of truth.
#
# Read by lib/harness/resolve.sh in pure bash, because hooks/sensitive-guard reads it and must work
# on a machine with no python3. That is why this is pipe-delimited text and not JSON.
#
# provides|<harness>|<primitive>|<source>|<version>|<date>
# requires|<gate>|<primitive>
#
# <source> is one of two kinds and says which. `vendor:` is a published artifact belonging to the
# harness. `probe:` is an observation somebody made and recorded. A keel file is neither: that keel
# ships a Stop hook is not evidence that the harness honours one, and a row citing only our own tree
# would keep asserting `provides` after the harness dropped the primitive. Nine rows were written
# that way on 2026-09-05 and every one was replaced.
#
# <version> is THE HARNESS's version, never keel's. keel doctor prints it beside the version of the
# harness it is running, and that comparison means nothing if this column holds 0.18.0.
#
# THE RULE THIS FILE EXISTS TO EXPRESS, which lives nowhere else in the tree: a gate is active on a
# harness IF AND ONLY IF that harness provides EVERY primitive the gate requires. So a `requires`
# row is not documentation. It is an instruction that silently disables that gate on every harness
# lacking the primitive, and adding one is the most consequential edit anybody makes here.
#
# A gate may carry more than one `requires` row, and `done-guard` does. A new gate needs both a file
# under hooks/ and at least one `requires` row; the test checks both directions and a missing half
# is a red build rather than a quiet hole.
#
# WHY THERE ARE THREE TRANSCRIPT PRIMITIVES, and there are still three. `transcript_path` is only
# the promise of a path. `transcript_turn_usage` is per-turn token usage, which lib/context_watch.py
# cannot work without. `transcript_turn_tool_calls` is the turn's tool calls, attributed to the
# agent that made them, which hooks/done-guard cannot work without. Three different promises, and
# none implies another. `subagent_transcript_path` is a fourth thing and NOT a transcript row field
# at all: it arrives on the SubagentStop event, so it is its own primitive with a vendor source.
# Bundling it into a transcript probe claimed evidence a transcript cannot carry, and zero of 368
# transcripts hold that key.
#
# WHAT "ATTRIBUTED" MEANS, AND WHY IT IS NOT "carries isSidechain". It said the latter until
# 2026-09-06, and that was the schema-in-the-name mistake of the paragraph below wearing a
# different hat: `isSidechain` is a Claude Code row field, so a primitive demanding one could only
# ever be held by Claude Code. What done-guard cannot function without is knowing that the calls it
# is reading are this agent's. Claude Code answers that with a per-row flag, because it interleaves
# a subagent's rows into the main transcript. Codex answers it by never interleaving: a subagent is
# its own thread with its own rollout, handed over as agent_transcript_path on SubagentStop, and
# every record carries its own thread id and turn id. That is strictly finer than a boolean, so it
# earns the primitive. The rule this settles, for the next harness: the primitive asks whether the
# turn's calls can be told from another agent's, never how.
#
# A PRIMITIVE NAMES A CAPABILITY, AND <source> IS WHERE ONE HARNESS'S SHAPE BELONGS. The Claude rows
# for these two cite message.usage and message.content[] tool_use blocks because that is how Claude
# Code satisfies them; a second harness satisfying the same two in a completely different format
# gets a row that is just as honest, and that is what lib/context_watch.py's two parsers exist to
# absorb. Until 2026-09-05 both were named after Claude Code's own transcript format, which put our
# schema in the primitive rather than in its evidence and so pinned Tier B at one gate by
# construction: no second harness could ever hold a row named for a wire format, however much
# evidence it had. A name that cannot be earned does not fail closed, it fails permanently.
# tests/test-harness-resolve.sh keeps schema words out of this column.
#
# Codex earned all three on 2026-09-06, in task 14 of the tiered multi-harness plan, so `done-guard`
# and `context-watch` resolve ACTIVE there and Tier B is three gates rather than one. The rows are
# probes against tests/fixtures/transcripts/codex.jsonl, a rollout captured from inside a live Stop
# hook, and the parsers that read it are lib/context_watch.py's _measure_codex and
# hooks/done-guard's codex_turn_tool_calls. Neither row was added before its parser worked against
# that capture: a row whose parser returns nothing is the "looks installed and is not" failure this
# file exists to prevent, dressed as evidence. ADR-0003 is the standing rule, absent evidence fails.
#
# `subagent_transcript_path` is PROVIDED and required by nothing, deliberately. done-guard reads it
# only on SubagentStop and falls back to transcript_path when it is absent (hooks/done-guard:219-220),
# so requiring it would disable the whole gate on a harness whose Stop path works. Under-requiring
# resolved done-guard active on Codex; over-requiring would resolve it inactive where it works. The
# rule is that a gate requires what it cannot function without, not everything it can use.
# Collapsing the three into one primitive resolved done-guard ACTIVE on Codex on the strength of
# stop_blocking alone, where it would have read a Codex transcript looking for Claude Code rows and
# failed open in silence. Caught in review on 2026-09-05, before it shipped, which is the whole
# point of the file.
#
# NOT EVERY PRIMITIVE IS A THING THE HARNESS DOES. `validated_word_ceiling` is the odd one and it is
# deliberate: it asks whether bodies held under keel's own 700 word ceiling have been VALIDATED on
# this harness, checked by eval arms to be followed there, not whether the harness offers anything.
# 700 is a policy limit (ADR-0011), not a measured skim threshold, so what an arm validates is the
# bodies at their lengths, not the number. So its evidence really is in our tree, which the
# `vendor:` and `probe:` paragraph above would otherwise forbid, and the distinction that saves it
# is that the rule there is about not citing keel as proof of a HARNESS behaviour. An eval arm we
# ran is an observation somebody made and recorded, so it is a probe like any other.
#
# There is deliberately NO codex row. Bodies under keel's word ceiling have been checked by arms
# only on Claude Code, and the 44-tokens-per-description figure was calibrated against its preload;
# Codex budgets a share of the context window instead and silently shortens or drops descriptions
# under pressure. So the ceiling is inherited and unvalidated there until S-19 runs arms there, and
# any document saying it holds on Codex is making a false claim today. The absence of the row is
# what fails that document's build.
#
# `pretooluse_agent_type` is whether a PreToolUse hook's input names the agent that made the call,
# and leaves it out for the main session. hooks/review-guard cannot tell a keel reviewer from the
# author without it, so it requires it, and Codex has no row because whether its hook input names
# the dispatched agent is unrecorded: the guard is dark there and the prompts keep the rule.
#
# ADR-0003: a primitive with no source, version and date is treated as not provided. Absent
# evidence fails, never passes. Do not add a row you have not checked.

